  "summary_md": "Soft housing and cautious consumer spending continue to pressure home-furnishings demand, but the morning’s most distinctive narratives center on companies breaking dramatically from the industry’s core. ATER has abandoned its legacy e‑commerce model entirely, selling off brands to pivot into mineral trading and African critical‑mineral exploration. XWIN is similarly recasting itself, launching an AI platform division and holding an indirect SpaceX interest atop its furniture base. At the opposite extreme, SNBR is fighting for survival, amending credit terms to avoid bankruptcy while betting on a mattress redesign.\n\nAcross the broader batch, defensive resilience and speculative repositioning sit side by side. COOK and FLXS are expanding margins despite flat-to-weaker unit volumes, while tariff uncertainty remains a recurring headwind for HOFT and FGI. A cluster of debt-free names—ETD, BSET, HOFT—are returning capital through buybacks and dividends, underscoring a flight to balance‑sheet conservatism. The most striking cross‑current is a widening divide between untested category leaps and a retreat into cash‑generative stability.",
